{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Sofia, the pertinent information is: sensory: sound and clothing-texture sensitivity; medical: none affecting school; behaviour: may hide under a desk when overloaded; and social: wants friends but misses subtle cues. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: unstructured group work; figurative or vague directions; fire alarms and cafeteria noise; and shifting away from a preferred topic. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Sofia, the most useful EA actions are to treat behaviour as communication; reduce sensory load before adding demands; use calm, concrete language; and teach peers inclusive ways to connect. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-G-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.4625, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "start": 1.475, "end": 1.75, "start_char": 22, "end_char": 26}, {"text